 (1973) dem-\nonstrated that catechol was 2 strong cocar-\nccinogen on mouse skin when applied together\nwith benzo(a)pyrene. These cocarcinogenic\nproperties have been recently confirmed by\nHecht ef al. (1981), who showed that cate-\nchol is one of the major cocarcinogens in the\nweakly acidic fraction of cigarette smoke\ncondensate. In addition, Morimoto and Wolff\n\n\u00bb Supported in par by coiteats from The Council\nfor Tobaceo Research-USA, Ine\nTo whom correspondence and cept requests should\n\nste, and excreted from mice exposed to whole cigarete smoke.\n\n(1980) have reported that catechol is a po-\ntent inducer of sister chromatid exchange\nand delays cell division in cultured human\nlymphocytes.\nIt has been suggested that reduetion of\ncatechol in cigarette smoke might be ex-\npected to lead to a smoke condensate with\nlower tumorigenicity (Hecht er al., 1981).\nAn investigation of the pharmacokinetics\nand metabolic fate of inhaled catechol would\nallow pharmacological analysis of its action\nand could provide additional information for\ncarcinogenicity experiments. In this paper,\nthe deposition and distribution of catechol\nin whole cigarette smoke were studied in\nBC3F1/Cum female mice.\n\nMETHODS.\n\nChemicals ond reagens. 'HJCatecbol\nHhlyoxybenzene) was syathesized and puried us:\n\n40s 0041-008 82/09040-10802,00/0\n\nCTR\n\n11246228\n\nCare ott Ase ro\n\u2018Aight weet sy frm tard\n\nCONTRACTS 026479", "words": [{"text": "7\\", "x0": 9, "y0": 9, "x1": 30, "y1": 34, "conf": 48.0}, {"text": "TOXICOLOGY", "x0": 173, "y0": 67, "x1": 229, "y1": 73, "conf": 51.0}, {"text": "AND", "x0": 232, "y0": 67, "x1": 250, "y1": 73, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "APPLIED", "x0": 253, "y0": 67, "x1": 288, "y1": 73, "conf": 86.0}, {"text": "PHARMACOLOGY", "x0": 291, "y0": 67, "x1": 377, "y1": 76, "conf": 91.0}, {"text": "64,", "x0": 365, "y0": 55, "x1": 377, "y1": 83, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "405-414", "x0": 381, "y0": 67, "x1": 417, "y1": 75, "conf": 91.0}, {"text": "(